more.args:
   loop
      call @parser(parser$get.token, token.type, token, keyword)
   until token.type = PARSER$END
      begin case
         case keyword = KW$NO.QUERY
            no.query = @true

* 14 Aug 26 Windows port - ADMINISTRATOR makes the account an SD administrator,
*   which since PROJECT_STATUS.md 5.6.1 means a Windows administrator.  Without
*   it the account is a standard local user, which is the safe default and the
*   common case.
*
*   Matched on the token text rather than on a KW$ constant, deliberately.  The
*   keyword numbers in SYSCOM/PARSER.H are a positional table of 216 entries
*   shared by every verb, and adding one to it to serve a single verb is a
*   larger and riskier change than this is worth.  The cost is that it cannot
*   be abbreviated, unlike NO.QUERY.
         case upcase(token) = 'ADMINISTRATOR'
            make.admin = @true
* 17 Aug 26 Windows port - AND AN ADMINISTRATOR GETS THE WHOLE VOC.  Before
*   section 8 this line was the ONLY thing ADMINISTRATOR did, and it touched
*   Windows group membership alone - every account got a byte-identical NEWVOC
*   whatever keyword was given.  That is the mechanical root of the finding in
*   section 8 that the two tiers which exist have identical real power.
*
*   OWNER'S RULING, 17 Aug 2026: "administrators get the whole voc".  It is not
*   a no-op and never was - NEWVOC has never held CREATE.ACCOUNT, DELETE.ACCOUNT
*   or the other seven, so before this an SD administrator had to LOGTO SDSYS to
*   administer anything.  They are safe to hand out because the programs behind
*   them gate themselves: $CREATEA (line 109 of this file), $DELACC, $MODIFYA,
*   $GRANTA and $UNLOCK each open with kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR, -1) and stop with
*   sysmsg(2001).  TWO OF THE NINE DO NOT, and are on the list anyway because
*   they are part of the administrative set the ruling names: UPDATE.ACCOUNT is
*   CPROC internal verb 15 and tests nothing, and ENCRYPT.FIELD points at
*   $CRYPTO, which does not exist in this tree at all - it is as broken in
*   SDSYS's own VOC as it will be here.
            tier = 'ADMINISTRATOR'

* 17 Aug 26 Windows port - PROGRAMMER, section 8.  A programmer works at the
*   prompt and builds things: compiler, cataloguer, editors, and the shell
*   verbs.  A standard account runs an application and gets none of them.
*
*   Matched on the token text for the same reason ADMINISTRATOR is, above: the
*   KW$ table in SYSCOM/PARSER.H is positional and shared by every verb, so
*   adding an entry to serve one verb is the larger and riskier change.  It
*   cannot be abbreviated, and that is the cost.
*
*   IT GRANTS RATHER THAN RESTRICTS, deliberately.  The default is the reduced
*   VOC, so an account created by somebody who has not read any of this is the
*   safe one; you have to ask for the capability.  And nothing here is a wall -
*   an administrator can copy any verb into any account's VOC afterwards
*   (owner, 17 Aug 2026), which is why this is a starting posture rather than
*   an enforcement boundary.
* 17 Aug 26 Windows port - AND IT MUST NOT DOWNGRADE.  Both keywords are
*   accepted on one command line and in either order, so PROGRAMMER given after
*   ADMINISTRATOR would otherwise take the administration verbs away again -
*   silently, because the account would still be a Windows administrator and
*   would still look right everywhere except its VOC.
         case upcase(token) = 'PROGRAMMER'
            if tier # 'ADMINISTRATOR' then tier = 'PROGRAMMER'

* 14 Aug 26 Windows port - ADOPT takes an OS account that already exists
*   instead of creating one.  It is the single exception to the owner's rule
*   of 14 Aug 2026 that only the installer's pre-existing OS account may be
*   given an SD account, and it exists so that the install can do exactly
*   that for the person installing.
*
*   GATED ON K$INTERNAL, not on K$ADMINISTRATOR, and the difference is the
*   whole point.  K$ADMINISTRATOR is every elevated session, which is every
*   administrator at a console; K$INTERNAL is set only by "sd -internal",
*   which is how the bootstrap and the install run.  So an administrator
*   cannot type this by hand and quietly attach an SD account to somebody
*   else's Windows login - the rule stays a rule at the console, and the
*   install gets its one door.  It is reported as an unrecognised token
*   rather than as a refusal, because a keyword a console user may never use
*   is not a keyword they need to know exists.
         case upcase(token) = 'ADOPT' and kernel(K$INTERNAL, -1)
            adopt = @true

         case 1
            stop sysmsg(2018, token) ;* Unexpected token (xx)
      end case
   repeat

* 17 Aug 26 Windows port - AN ADOPTED ACCOUNT IS AN ADMINISTRATOR'S, section 8.
*   ADOPT is reachable only from sd -internal, which today means one caller:
*   gplbld/adopt-account.ps1 giving the install's own user an SD account (step
*   1f).  That person is a Windows administrator by definition - Inno requires
*   elevation to install - and PROJECT_STATUS.md 5.6.1 settled that a Windows
*   administrator is an SD administrator.  So their SD account gets an
*   administrator's VOC.
*
*   WITHOUT THIS THE INSTALLING USER LANDS STANDARD, with no BASIC, no ED and no
*   CATALOG, because adopt-account.ps1 passes no tier keyword and never had one
*   to pass.  That is a regression the tier work itself would have introduced,
*   and it would have shown up as "SD is broken after installing it".
*
*   A DEFAULT, NOT AN OVERRIDE.  It applies only when no keyword was given, so
*   an explicit PROGRAMMER on the same line still wins.  make.admin is
*   deliberately untouched: ADOPT changes nothing about the Windows account, and
*   the person is already in Administrators.
   if adopt and tier = 'STANDARD' then tier = 'ADMINISTRATOR'
return
